         Example of computing a table for an expansion-contraction.              
         The table type is always 14 and therefore is not an input               
         item.  Two tables are produced.  One for flow from                      
         upstream to downstream and one for downstream to upstream.              
         The lines UP, DN, UD, DU must always appear in the order                
         given.  If reverse flow will not occur, leave the table number          
         in the reverse direction blank.  Then only one table will be            
         computed.    The distance between the sections can be given             
         as zero.  In this case FEQUTL ignores the boundary friction             
         losses.                                                                 
  
