         Example of using EMBANKQ to compute flows for non-embankment            
         shaped weirs.  EMBANKQ does not really know what the weir looks         
         like.  All it knows is the following:                                   
  
          1. When the ratio of the approach piezometric head to the              
             width of the weir crest is less than HLCRIT then the                
             flow is considered to be low head flow.   Then EMBANKQ              
             uses the low head tables to define the weir coefficients            
             using as an argument the total head of the approach                 
             flow.                                                               
  
          2. When the ratio of the approach piezometric head to the              
             width of the weir crest is greater or equal to HLCRIT then          
             the flow is considered to be high head flow.  Then EMBANKQ          
             uses the high head tables to define the weir coefficients           
             using as an argument the piezometric head to width                  
             ratio.                                                              
  
          3. EMBANKQ always uses the total head(piezometric head                 
             plus velocity head) in the weir flow equation.  If the              
             APPROACH elevation is made large and negative the                   
             velocity head is negligible.  Thus you have control over            
             the use of velocity head by adjusting the approach                  
             elevation.                                                          
  
           4. The designations of GRAVEL and PAVED are only used to              
              distinguish between two sets of tables.  Again                     
              EMBANKQ does not know the nature of the surface and the            
              only effect of the nature of the surface is to change              
              tables used to define the weir coefficients and the                
              submergence.                                                       
  
         Thus it is possible to select the correct tables and width to           
         make EMBANKQ compute a table for many different weir shapes.            
  
         The following example computes the flow over a weir with a              
         crest that is 6. inches wide with both its faces                        
         vertical.  This is a classical example of a weir which at               
         low head  behaves as a broad crested weir and                           
         at higher heads behaves as a sharp crested weir.                        
         In this case the weir coefficient                                       
         was a function of the ratio of the piezometric head  to the             
         width.  Thus I set HLCRIT to 0.0 so that all flows are high             
         head flows.  Thus the low head tables, although input, would            
         not be used by EMBANKQ.  The weir coefficients have been                
         taken from Table 5-3, p. 5-40 of Handbook of Hydraulics.                
         6th edition, by Brater and King.  It is unclear from that               
         source if the coefficients were based on the inclusion or               
         exclusion of the approach velocity head. Because they seem              
         to be independent of weir height, I assumed that the                    
         total head is to be used in the computation.  Therefore                 
         the APPROACH is set to yield an approach velocity.                      
  
         I also set HLMAX to a large number so I do not get warning              
         messages that the ratio of head to width exceeds that established       
         by experiment.                                                          
  
         Submergence for weirs like this is difficult.  The problem              
         arises in that the weir behaves differently at different heads          
         and EMBANKQ does not try to be that precise about submergence.          
         Since the weir will behave like a sharp crested weir at moderate        
         heads,  I elected to treat submergence like that for a sharp            
         crested weir.   Equation 5-50 from Brater and King, 6-th Ed.            
         was used with n=1.5                                                     
  
  
         If the weir coefficient is not a function of the ratio of               
         the approach piezometric head to the crest width but is                 
         a function of the approach piezometric head, then set the               
         crest width to 1.0 so that the head and the ratio are                   
         the same.  Also set HLCRIT to 0.0 so that the high head                 
         tables will be used.  Then place the proper coefficients in             
         the high head tables.                                                   
  
         If the weir coefficient should be a function of the approach            
         total head, then set HLCRIT to a large value so that only               
         the low head table will be used.  Then place the proper                 
         coefficients in the low head tables.                                    
  
